css-background-pattern-generator.formula:
.pattern {
  background-color: <base color>;
  background-image: <gradient layers>;
  background-size: <tile width> <tile height>;
}

Dots: radial-gradient(circle, FG r, transparent r+1)
Grid: linear-gradient(FG 1px, transparent 1px) + linear-gradient(90deg, ...)
Stripes: repeating-linear-gradient(45deg, FG 0 half, transparent half full)
Checker: conic-gradient(FG 25%, transparent 0 50%, FG 0 75%, transparent 0)

css-background-pattern-generator.workedExample:
Pattern: Dots, pattern color #4f46e5, background #ffffff, tile size 24px.
Generated CSS:
.my-pattern {
  background-color: #ffffff;
  background-image: radial-gradient(circle, #4f46e5 6px, transparent 7px);
  background-size: 24px 24px;
}
The dot radius is one quarter of the tile (24 / 4 = 6px), so raising the tile size to 48px would double the dot to 12px and halve the pattern density.

css-box-shadow-generator.formula:
box-shadow: [inset] <offset-x> <offset-y> <blur> <spread> <color>;

Example: box-shadow: 0px 8px 24px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25);
- offset-x / offset-y: shadow position (can be negative)
- blur: softness of the edge (0 = hard edge)
- spread: grows (+) or shrinks (−) the shadow
- color: any CSS color; rgba with low alpha looks most natural

css-checkbox-generator.formula:
.fancy-check input { position: absolute; opacity: 0; }   /* real input stays in DOM */
.fancy-check .box { width: S; height: S; border: W solid BC; border-radius: S × corner%; }
.fancy-check input:checked + .box { background: ACCENT; border-color: ACCENT; }
.fancy-check .box::after { /* checkmark */ border-width: 0 W W 0; transform: rotate(45deg); }

Switch: track width = 2 × S, knob = S − 4, travel = track − knob − 4

